 and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=8,Y=1)=f(8,1)=0.27.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=8)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.45\\cdot 0.6=0.27.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =0.22=0.55\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,1) & =0.33=0.55\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(8,-1) & =0.18=0.45\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163617/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162744"] :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-143331/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a5f6e4e2-9922-5bd2-88c6-37c6e78e861e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143331/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4e57c398-7536-5f02-956f-8819449c0266" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143331/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2e5d965a-1af1-549b-a2f4-e81dd3208989" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143331/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "06293fc4-9606-5e68-872b-9f1f16ac22cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143331/selection/c5361