f8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-110385"] :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1) & ={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.5\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-143317/selection/c2dc451a54309d3339d4fbd1e2ea5a56b284f10492dac356ca0dcf0295b0f9e6" :answer/id #uuid "96b07ab5-1360-56b8-87a6-766301bb826b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.06" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143317/selection/3cbdf95cfd5e40a66d9c06a25fae4f2b1ebc35cb68cd1a8c8fced199bacffd13" :answer/id #uuid "97e7a0dd-4c48-57b8-b0a8-b7e1d345f336" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143317/selection/2468d3f08d897d95f4d28b48c13e6034ce1716985d166fd89a0b8119476fe5c8" :answer/id #uuid "c7f01881-8a5d-5aca-aa89-7656519